    "script": "[Prof. Park] Look at Board Work #4: $\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{5}{12}$. Notice something special about the numbers 4 and 12?\n\n[TA Sora] Yes! 12 is already a multiple of 4 ($4 \\times 3 = 12$)! That means we do NOT need to touch the second fraction at all!\n\n[Prof. Park] Exactly! The LCD is simply $12$. Many students needlessly multiply $4 \\times 12 = 48$ and get stuck in huge numbers.\n\n[TA Sora] We only change $\\frac{3}{4}$ by multiplying top and bottom by $3$, giving $\\frac{9}{12}$.\n\n[Prof. Park] Subtracting $9 - 5$ yields $\\frac{4}{12}$. And Sora, what must every student do next?\n\n[TA Sora] Reduce! Divide top and bottom by 4 to get $\\frac{1}{3}$. Never stop at $\\frac{4}{12}$!"
