    "script": "[Prof. Park] Board Work #5 is a true multi-step master problem: $(\\frac{4}{7} - \\frac{2}{3})^2$.\n\n[TA Sora] Oh, this combines fractions, signed numbers, AND exponents all in one problem!\n\n[Prof. Park] Order of operations is our guiding light: Parentheses first. We must subtract $\\frac{4}{7} - \\frac{2}{3}$ before we ever think about the exponent $2$.\n\n[TA Sora] Finding the LCD of 7 and 3 gives 21. $\\frac{12}{21} - \\frac{14}{21} = -\\frac{2}{21}$. Notice the negative sign!\n\n[Prof. Park] Now we square $(-\\frac{2}{21})^2$. A negative times a negative is positive: $(-2)^2 = 4$, and $21^2 = 441$.\n\n[TA Sora] Final answer: $\\frac{4}{441}$! Pure mathematical triumph!"
