    "num": 6,
    "type": "math_problem",
    "slideTypeLabel": "Workbook Page 7: Rows 7 & 8",
    "title": "Section 1.1 Phrases #7 & #8: Cubes, Opposites & Products",
    "subtitle": "Unit 1 • Lecture 04 • Section 1.1 Table rows 7 & 8 (Workbook p. 7)",
    "detail": "Lecture 04: Translating English Phrases into Algebraic Expressions",
    "instructor": "Prof. Eunju Park • TA Sora (Gallatin College MSU)",
    "problem": "**Translate into algebraic expressions:**\n\n7. **Workbook Phrase #7:** \"the difference between a number cubed and the opposite of that number\"\n8. **Workbook Phrase #8:** \"the product of three numbers\"",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Phrase \\#7: } & \\text{\"the difference between a number cubed and the opposite of that number\"} \\\\\n& \\text{1. A number cubed: } x^3 \\\\\n& \\text{2. The opposite of that number: } -x \\\\\n& \\text{3. Difference between them: } x^3 - (-x) = \\mathbf{x^3 + x} \\\\[1em]\n\\text{Phrase \\#8: } & \\text{\"the product of three numbers\"} \\\\\n& \\text{Three distinct numbers require three variables multiplied together: } \\mathbf{xyz} \\quad (\\text{or } abc)\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Sora's Pro-Tip on \"Opposite\":**\n- \"The opposite of a number\" always means $-x$.\n- Subtraction of an opposite creates a double negative: $x^3 - (-x) = x^3 + x$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 6 brings us to Phrases #7 and #8. Phrase #7: 'The product of negative five and the cube of a number.'\n\n[TA Sora] 'Product' means multiplication between two quantities: $-5$ and 'the cube of a number'.\n\n[Prof. Park] What is the cube of a number $x$?\n\n[TA Sora] That's $x$ raised to the 3rd power: $x^3$! Multiply by $-5$: $-5x^3$!\n\n[Prof. Park] Now look at Phrase #8: 'The opposite of a number, plus eight.' Sora, how do we translate 'the opposite of a number'?\n\n[TA Sora] In algebra, 'opposite' simply means change the signâput a negative sign in front! So the opposite of $x$ is $-x$.\n\n[Prof. Park] And 'plus eight' is $+ 8$.\n\n[TA Sora] So the complete translation is $-x + 8$! Or by commutativity, $8 - x$! Both are 100% correct!\n\n[Prof. Park] Notice how algebraic translation trains your brain to break down ambiguous human language into precise, logical components.\n\n[TA Sora] That skill goes way beyond mathematics: in law, computer programming, and business contracts, parsing exact meanings is everything!\n\n[Prof. Park] Keep that principle close at hand as we move forward.\n\n[TA Sora] Absolutely, Professor! On to the next challenge!\n\n[Prof. Park] When students encounter complex word problems on midterms, what is the best strategy to avoid feeling overwhelmed?\n\n[TA Sora] Read the sentence through three times! First for the story, second to circle the operation signal words, and third to write the algebraic symbols! Patience and method always win!"
