    "slideTypeLabel": "Workbook Page 7: Rows 9 & 10",
    "title": "Section 1.1 Phrases #9 & #10: Multiple Variables & Fraction Multipliers",
    "subtitle": "Unit 1 • Lecture 04 • Section 1.1 Table rows 9 & 10 (Workbook p. 7)",
    "detail": "Lecture 04: Translating English Phrases into Algebraic Expressions",
    "instructor": "Prof. Eunju Park • TA Sora (Gallatin College MSU)",
    "problem": "**Translate into algebraic expressions:**\n\n9. **Workbook Phrase #9:** \"five times a number reduced by the square of a second number\"\n10. **Workbook Phrase #10:** \"three-fourths of a number increased by one hundred\"",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Phrase \\#9: } & \\text{\"five times a number reduced by the square of a second number\"} \\\\\n& \\text{1. First number: } x \\implies \\text{\"five times a number\" } = 5x \\\\\n& \\text{2. Second number: } y \\implies \\text{\"square of a second number\" } = y^2 \\\\\n& \\text{3. \"Reduced by\" means subtraction: } \\mathbf{5x - y^2} \\\\[1em]\n\\text{Phrase \\#10: } & \\text{\"three-fourths of a number increased by one hundred\"} \\\\\n& \\text{1. \"Three-fourths of a number\" } = \\dfrac{3}{4}x \\\\\n& \\text{2. \"Increased by one hundred\" } \\implies \\mathbf{\\dfrac{3}{4}x + 100}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**CRITICAL DISTINCTION:**\n- In Phrase #9, \"reduced by\" maintains left-to-right order ($5x - y^2$). It is NOT a reversal phrase like \"less than\"!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 7 introduces two different variables in Phrases #9 and #10! Phrase #9: 'Five times the sum of $x$ and $y$.'\n\n[TA Sora] Here is that grouping signal again: 'Five times THE SUM OF...' That means 5 multiplies the entire grouped sum!\n\n[Prof. Park] So we write 5 in front of parentheses containing $x + y$: $5(x + y)$!\n\n[TA Sora] If you wrote $5x + y$, the 5 would only multiply $x$, leaving $y$ stranded! Parentheses are required.\n\n[Prof. Park] Now examine Phrase #10: 'Two-thirds of a number, minus fourteen.' Sora, what does the word 'of' mean after a fraction?\n\n[TA Sora] 'Of' means MULTIPLY! Two-thirds of $x$ means $\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot x$, or $\\frac{2x}{3}$!\n\n[Prof. Park] And 'minus fourteen' is $- 14$.\n\n[TA Sora] Full expression: $\\frac{2}{3}x - 14$! Clean, precise, and unambiguous!\n\n[Prof. Park] Notice how algebraic translation trains your brain to break down ambiguous human language into precise, logical components.\n\n[TA Sora] That skill goes way beyond mathematics: in law, computer programming, and business contracts, parsing exact meanings is everything!\n\n[Prof. Park] Keep that principle close at hand as we move forward.\n\n[TA Sora] Absolutely, Professor! On to the next challenge!\n\n[Prof. Park] When students encounter complex word problems on midterms, what is the best strategy to avoid feeling overwhelmed?\n\n[TA Sora] Read the sentence through three times! First for the story, second to circle the operation signal words, and third to write the algebraic symbols! Patience and method always win!"
