    "subtitle": "Unit 1 • Lecture 04 • Section 1.1 Table rows 11 & 12 (Workbook p. 7)",
    "detail": "Lecture 04: Translating English Phrases into Algebraic Expressions",
    "instructor": "Prof. Eunju Park • TA Sora (Gallatin College MSU)",
    "problem": "**Translate into algebraic expressions:**\n\n11. **Workbook Phrase #11:** \"fourteen less than half of a number\"\n12. **Workbook Phrase #12:** \"two-thirds of a number\"",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Phrase \\#11: } & \\text{\"fourteen less than half of a number\"} \\\\\n& \\text{1. \"Half of a number\" } = \\dfrac{1}{2}x \\quad \\left(\\text{or } \\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\\n& \\text{2. \"Fourteen less than\" } \\implies \\text{REVERSAL: subtract 14 from half: } \\mathbf{\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 14} \\\\[1em]\n\\text{Phrase \\#12: } & \\text{\"two-thirds of a number\"} \\\\\n& \\text{\"Of\" means multiplication: } \\mathbf{\\dfrac{2}{3}x} \\quad \\left(\\text{or } \\dfrac{2x}{3}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**THE REVERSAL TRAP IN PHRASE #11:**\n- Writing $14 - \\frac{1}{2}x$ is the most frequent student error on page 7.\n- Remember: \"14 less than\" means $- 14$ at the end: $\\mathbf{\\frac{1}{2}x - 14}$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 8 tests our skills with Phrases #11 and #12. Phrase #11: 'Four less than the quotient of a number and six.'\n\n[TA Sora] I hear two sirens! First siren: 'less than'âthat means REVERSAL! The 4 is being subtracted at the very end!\n\n[Prof. Park] And second siren: 'the quotient of a number and six'. What is that?\n\n[TA Sora] That's $\\frac{x}{6}$! Now apply the reversal: subtract 4 from that quotient!\n\n[Prof. Park] So we get $\\frac{x}{6} - 4$! Notice if someone rushed, they would write $4 - \\frac{x}{6}$, which is completely backwards!\n\n[TA Sora] Now look at Phrase #12: 'The ratio of seven and the difference of a number and two.'\n\n[Prof. Park] 'Ratio' means fraction bar! 7 is on top. What is on the bottom, Sora?\n\n[TA Sora] 'The difference of a number and two', which is $(x - 2)$! So the whole fraction is $\\frac{7}{x - 2}$!\n\n[Prof. Park] Notice how algebraic translation trains your brain to break down ambiguous human language into precise, logical components.\n\n[TA Sora] That skill goes way beyond mathematics: in law, computer programming, and business contracts, parsing exact meanings is everything!\n\n[Prof. Park] Keep that principle close at hand as we move forward.\n\n[TA Sora] Absolutely, Professor! On to the next challenge!\n\n[Prof. Park] When students encounter complex word problems on midterms, what is the best strategy to avoid feeling overwhelmed?\n\n[TA Sora] Read the sentence through three times! First for the story, second to circle the operation signal words, and third to write the algebraic symbols! Patience and method always win!"
