    "script": "[Prof. Park] Moving to slide 7 with Example 5 Parts C and D: Part C: (a^9 b^6)^5. Part D: (w^3 Â· w^8) / (w^5)^2. In Part C, (a^9 b^6)^5 has two variable factors inside. Distribute the outside exponent 5 to both: For a: (a^9)^5 = a^(9 Â· 5) = a^45.\n\n[TA Sora] For b: (b^6)^5 = b^(6 Â· 5) = b^30. So our simplified term is a^45 b^30! Now look at Part D: (w^3 Â· w^8) / (w^5)^2. Here we combine the product rule, the power of a power rule, and the quotient rule!\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's simplify top and bottom separately: In the numerator: w^3 Â· w^8 = w^(3 + 8) = w^11. In the denominator: (w^5)^2 = w^(5 Â· 2) = w^10. Now we have w^11 / w^10. Apply the quotient rule: 11 - 10 = 1.\n\n[TA Sora] So w^11 / w^10 = w^1 = w! Look how an expression with four exponents collapsed down to a single letter w. That is mathematical elegance in action. If someone tested this with w = 2: numerator would be 2^3 Â· 2^8 = 8 Â· 256 = 2048.\n\n[Prof. Park] Denominator: (2^5)^2 = 32^2 = 1024. 2048 divided by 1024 is exactly 2! The algebra works flawlessly with actual numbers.\n\n[TA Sora] Here is an extra pro-tip for slide 7: whenever you have a fraction with multiple variables and coefficients raised to a negative power outside, you can also flip the ENTIRE fraction upside down right at the start to turn the outer exponent positive!\n\n[Prof. Park] That is the Fraction Inversion Property: (A/B)^(-n) = (B/A)^n! In our problem, flipping the fraction first gives [ z^(-4) / (3 x^(-2) y) ]^3, which leads to the exact same final answer: x^6 / (27 y^3 z^12).\n\n[TA Sora] Having two independent ways to solve a problem is the greatest confidence builder in algebra!"
