    "script": "[Prof. Park] Now let's tackle Example 5 Part E on slide 8: Simplify fully: [ (2 x^6 w^5) / (3 x w^5) ]^3.\n\n[TA Sora] When you have a fraction inside parentheses raised to an outside power, you have two choices: Choice 1: Distribute the power 3 to every single factor on top and bottom first. Choice 2: SIMPLIFY INSIDE FIRST, then raise to the power 3!\n\n[Prof. Park] Sora's Golden Rule: ALWAYS simplify inside the parentheses first! It shrinks your numbers before you raise them to powers. Let's follow Sora's advice and simplify inside first: Look at the w terms: w^5 on top and w^5 on the bottom. w^5 / w^5 = 1! They cancel out completely!\n\n[TA Sora] Look at the x terms: x^6 on top and x^1 on the bottom. By the quotient rule: 6 - 1 = 5, so x^5 remains on top. The coefficients 2 and 3 cannot be reduced. So inside the parentheses, we are left with: (2x^5 / 3)!\n\n[Prof. Park] Look how much smaller that is! Now apply the outside cube 3 to everything: Numerator: 2^3 Â· (x^5)^3 = 8 x^(5 Â· 3) = 8x^15. Denominator: 3^3 = 27. Our final answer is: (8x^15) / 27.\n\n[TA Sora] If we had distributed the cube first, we would have had 2^3 = 8, x^18, w^15 over 27 x^3 w^15, and then canceled. Simplifying inside first was much cleaner and saved us two full steps!\n\n[Prof. Park] Take your time with every step here. Writing out intermediate lines is the secret to 100% accuracy!\n\n[Prof. Park] Exactly! Protect your signs, trust the algebra rules, and you will get the correct answer every single time!"
