    "script": "[Prof. Park] Now we arrive at the ultimate master problem on slide 9: Example 5 Part F on workbook page 11: Simplify fully: [ (3 x^11 y^5) / (2 x^(-1) y^8) ]^(-2). This is the grand championship problem of Section 1.2!\n\n[TA Sora] It has coefficients, positive exponents, negative exponents inside, and an outside negative exponent. Let's attack it methodically using our three-step protocol: Step 1: Simplify inside the parentheses first! Let's do that! Look at x: we have x^11 on top and x^(-1) on the bottom.\n\n[Prof. Park] The x^(-1) on the bottom takes the elevator upstairs to the numerator and becomes x^(+1)! Upstairs, we already have x^11, so x^11 Â· x^1 = x^(11 + 1) = x^12. Now look at y: y^5 on top and y^8 on the bottom.\n\n[TA Sora] 8 - 5 = 3, so y^3 stays on the bottom. So inside our parentheses, we now have: (3 x^12) / (2 y^3). Step 2: Look at the outside exponent: it is -2!\n\n[Prof. Park] The negative sign on the outside exponent flips the entire inside fraction to its reciprocal: [ (2 y^3) / (3 x^12) ]^(+2)! Step 3: Now square every factor inside: Numerator: 2^2 Â· (y^3)^2 = 4 y^(3 Â· 2) = 4 y^6.\n\n[TA Sora] Denominator: 3^2 Â· (x^12)^2 = 9 x^(12 Â· 2) = 9 x^24. Assemble the final fraction: (4 y^6) / (9 x^24)! Every exponent is positive, coefficients 4 and 9 share no common factors, and each variable appears once. Perfection!\n\n[Prof. Park] Take your time with every step here. Writing out intermediate lines is the secret to 100% accuracy!\n\n[Prof. Park] Exactly! Protect your signs, trust the algebra rules, and you will get the correct answer every single time!"
