    "script": "[Prof. Park] What a triumph! On slide 10, we synthesize all 5 examples from Section 1.2 into our Master Summary Table. Sora, let's review the final golden rules. 1. Zero Exponent: a^0 = 1 (for non-zero base). 2. Product Rule: multiplying identical bases -> ADD exponents. 3.\n\n[TA Sora] Quotient Rule: dividing identical bases -> SUBTRACT exponents. 4. Negative Exponents: the elevator ruleâmove across the fraction bar to turn positive. 5. Power of a Power: multiply exponents. 6. Power of a Fraction: simplify inside first, flip if the outside power is negative, then distribute to every factor.\n\n[Prof. Park] Mastering these exponent laws is essential because they form the operational foundation for polynomial multiplication in Section 1.3, FOIL in Section 1.4, rational expressions in Section 1.5, and all factoring techniques in Unit 2. In Lecture 07, we advance to Section 1.3 on workbook page 12: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials.\n\n[TA Sora] Outstanding work today, everyone, and we will see you in Lecture 07! Let us also review the difference between (-x)^2 and -x^2 one last time before we leave exponents: In (-3)^2, the negative is inside parentheses, so (-3)(-3) = +9. In -3^2, the negative is OUTSIDE, so -(3)(3) = -9!\n\n[Prof. Park] Keep this distinction on a sticky note on your computer screen!\n\n[TA Sora] Take your time with every step here. Writing out intermediate lines is the secret to 100% accuracy!\n\n[TA Sora] Exactly! Protect your signs, trust the algebra rules, and you will get the correct answer every single time!"
