    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Like Terms: } & 5x^2 \\text{ and } 3x^2 \\implies (5+3)x^2 = \\mathbf{8x^2} \\\\\n\\text{Unlike Terms: } & 5x^2 \\text{ and } 3x \\implies \\text{CANNOT be combined!}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Sora's Warning:** When adding like terms, NEVER add or change the exponents! $3x^2 + 5x^2 = 8x^2$, NOT $8x^4$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Welcome everyone to Lecture 07 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the desk is TA Sora. Today, we open Section 1.3 of our course workbook, found on page 12: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials. Hello everyone!\n\n[TA Sora] Today we transition from working with single-term monomials to working with polynomialsâexpressions that contain multiple terms combined by addition and subtraction.\n\n[Prof. Park] In the real world, polynomials are the mathematical models behind everything from cost and revenue curves in business, to trajectory curves of aircraft landing at Bozeman Yellowstone International Airport, to stress loads on construction framing in Gallatin Valley residential builds.\n\n[TA Sora] For example, a business in Belgrade might model its total revenue as R(x) = 150x - 0.5x^2, and its production costs as C(x) = 3000 + 40x. To find profit, they subtract the cost polynomial from the revenue polynomial: P(x) = R(x) - C(x)!\n\n[Prof. Park] Understanding how to classify, add, subtract, and distribute polynomials is the foundational engine of business, engineering, and data science. Let's review the essential vocabulary on slide 1: A monomial has exactly 1 term (like 5x^3). A binomial has exactly 2 terms (like 2x + 7).\n\n[TA Sora] A trinomial has exactly 3 terms (like 4x^2 - 5x + 9). And any algebraic expression composed of one or more terms is a polynomial. Each term consists of a numerical coefficient and variable factors.\n\n[Prof. Park] The degree of a single term is the sum of the exponents on its variables: for instance, 4x^3 has degree 3, while 7x^2 y^4 has degree 2 + 4 = 6! The degree of an entire polynomial is the highest degree of any term present.\n\n[TA Sora] Sora, what is the golden requirement for combining terms in a polynomial? LIKE TERMS ONLY! Two terms are 'like terms' if and only if they have the exact same variable raised to the exact same exponent! For example, 5x^2 and 3x^2 are like terms because both have x squared.\n\n[Prof. Park] But 5x^2 and 5x are NOT like terms because one has power 2 and the other has power 1. When you combine like terms, you add or subtract the numerical coefficients, and the variable part remains completely unchanged. Let's practice on slide 2!"
