    "script": "[Prof. Park] Let's examine Section 1.3 Examples 1A and 1B on workbook page 12: Part A: Simplify 6y + 12 - 7y. Part B: Simplify -3x^2 + 5x + 9x^2 - 2. In Part A, we have three terms: 6y, +12, and -7y.\n\n[TA Sora] Let's identify the like terms: 6y and -7y both have the variable y to the first power. The constant 12 is on its own. Combine the like terms by adding their numerical coefficients: 6 - 7 = -1.\n\n[Prof. Park] So 6y - 7y = -1y, which in algebra we write simply as -y. Now bring along the constant +12: our simplified expression is -y + 12 (or 12 - y). Notice how the variable y didn't change at allâit did not become y squared, and it did not disappear.\n\n[TA Sora] Only the count of how many y's we have changed from 6 and -7 to -1. Now look at Part B: -3x^2 + 5x + 9x^2 - 2. Here we have terms of degree 2, degree 1, and degree 0 (the constant).\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's group the degree 2 terms: -3x^2 and +9x^2. Combine their coefficients: -3 + 9 = +6, so we have 6x^2. Now look for other degree 1 terms: we have +5x, but no other x terms to combine with, so it stays +5x.\n\n[TA Sora] And the constant -2 is alone, so it stays -2. Assembling in descending order of exponents gives: 6x^2 + 5x - 2. Why do we write it in descending order? Writing polynomials in descending degree orderâhighest power first, down to the constantâis standard mathematical etiquette.\n\n[Prof. Park] It makes it easy to identify the leading coefficient, which is 6, the degree of the polynomial, which is 2 (a quadratic trinomial), and ensures everyone grading your paper can immediately confirm your answer.\n\n[TA Sora] You can also align them in vertical columns on your paper: place degree 2 terms in one column, degree 1 in the next, and constants at the end, just like multi-digit addition in grade school!"
