    "script": "[Prof. Park] Moving to slide 3, let's tackle Examples 1C and 1D: Part C: 3n^2 - 17n - n^2 + 4n. Part D: 4ab^2 + 6a^2 b - 8ab^2 + 2a^2 b. In Part C, notice the term -n^2.\n\n[TA Sora] Whenever a variable appears with a negative sign but no written number in front, its invisible coefficient is 1! So -n^2 is really -1n^2. Always write that little '1' with your pencil if you are prone to skipping it.\n\n[Prof. Park] Now let's group the n^2 terms: 3n^2 - 1n^2 = (3 - 1)n^2 = 2n^2. Next, group the linear n terms: -17n + 4n = (-17 + 4)n = -13n. Combine them: 2n^2 - 13n.\n\n[TA Sora] Students often ask: 'Can I combine 2n^2 - 13n into -11n?' Absolutely not! n^2 and n are completely different dimensionsâone is an area of size n by n, and the other is a length of size n. They cannot be combined!\n\n[Prof. Park] Now look at Part Dâthis is a classic exam trap: 4ab^2 + 6a^2 b - 8ab^2 + 2a^2 b. Sora, both terms contain a and b, so why aren't all four of them like terms? Because the exponents on the individual variables are different!\n\n[TA Sora] In 4ab^2, the b is squared: a^1 b^2. In 6a^2 b, the a is squared: a^2 b^1. Think of construction materials: ab^2 is a board of width a and length b squared, while a^2 b is a board of width a squared and length b!\n\n[Prof. Park] They have different geometric shapes and cannot be stacked together. Let's combine matching categories: For the ab^2 group: 4ab^2 - 8ab^2 = (4 - 8)ab^2 = -4ab^2. For the a^2 b group: 6a^2 b + 2a^2 b = (6 + 2)a^2 b = +8a^2 b.\n\n[TA Sora] Our final answer is: -4ab^2 + 8a^2 b (or 8a^2 b - 4ab^2). Always match the exponent to the specific letter!"
