    "script": "[Prof. Park] Now let's explore the Distributive Property with Monomials on slide 5: Examples 3A and 3B on workbook page 13: Part A: -2x(3x^2 + 8x - 4). Part B: 4x^2(2x^3 - 5x + 7). The Distributive Property says: a(b + c + d) = ab + ac + ad.\n\n[TA Sora] The monomial sitting outside multiplies every single term inside the parentheses! Think of a physical rectangular field in Gallatin County partitioned into three sections: the common width is -2x, and the three lengths are 3x^2, 8x, and -4. The total area is the sum of the three individual section areas!\n\n[Prof. Park] And remember: when multiplying variable terms, we use the product rule from Lecture 05âwe MULTIPLY the coefficients and we ADD the exponents! Let's distribute -2x to each of the three terms in Part A: First term: (-2x)(3x^2). Multiply coefficients: -2 Â· 3 = -6.\n\n[TA Sora] Add exponents: x^1 Â· x^2 = x^3. Result: -6x^3. Second term: (-2x)(8x). Multiply coefficients: -2 Â· 8 = -16. Add exponents: x^1 Â· x^1 = x^2. Result: -16x^2. Third term: (-2x)(-4). Negative times negative is positive! (-2)(-4) = +8, with x attached: +8x.\n\n[Prof. Park] Assemble the three products: -6x^3 - 16x^2 + 8x. Notice the signs flipped completely: positive 3x^2 became negative, positive 8x became negative, and negative 4 became positive! Now let's do Part B: 4x^2(2x^3 - 5x + 7). Term 1: (4x^2)(2x^3) = (4 Â· 2) x^(2 + 3) = 8x^5.\n\n[TA Sora] Term 2: (4x^2)(-5x^1) = (4 Â· -5) x^(2 + 1) = -20x^3. Term 3: (4x^2)(7) = (4 Â· 7) x^2 = +28x^2. Combine them: 8x^5 - 20x^3 + 28x^2. Notice that there are no like terms among x^5, x^3, and x^2, so this polynomial is fully simplified.\n\n[Prof. Park] Think of residential construction framing across Bozeman and Belgrade: when contractors calculate structural perimeter lumber or roof truss dimensions with varying spans, variables represent modular lumber dimensions. Missing terms just mean zero length in that specific componentâthey do not vanish or break the formula!\n\n[TA Sora] That is why I tell students to write in placeholder zeros if it helps them stay aligned on paper: write + 0x^3 as a placeholder column. That way, degree 4, 3, 2, 1, and 0 terms line up in neat vertical columns like an accounting ledger!"
