    "title": "Example 3E & 3F: Nested Parentheses Simplification",
    "subtitle": "Unit 1 • Lecture 07 • Section 1.3 Example 3E & 3F (Workbook p. 13)",
    "detail": "Lecture 07: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials & The Distributive Property",
    "instructor": "Prof. Eunju Park • TA Sora (Gallatin College MSU)",
    "problem": "### Simplify fully by clearing nested grouping symbols (Workbook p. 13)\n$$\\text{E. } 3 + 2(5x - 4(x - 9)) + 7x$$\n$$\\text{F. } 11x - 8(y + 5(y - 2(y - x)))$$",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{Part\\ E:}\\quad & \\text{Innermost first: } -4(x - 9) = -4x + 36 \\\\\n& 3 + 2(5x - 4x + 36) + 7x = 3 + 2(x + 36) + 7x \\\\\n&= 3 + 2x + 72 + 7x = \\mathbf{9x + 75} \\\\[1em]\n\\mathbf{Part\\ F:}\\quad & \\text{Innermost: } -2(y - x) = -2y + 2x \\\\\n&= 11x - 8(y + 5(y - 2y + 2x)) \\\\\n&= 11x - 8(y + 5(-y + 2x)) \\\\\n&= 11x - 8(y - 5y + 10x) = 11x - 8(-4y + 10x) \\\\\n&= 11x + 32y - 80x = \\mathbf{-69x + 32y}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Order of Operations:** In 3E, NEVER add $3 + 2 = 5$ at the start! Multiplication takes precedence over addition!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] To conclude Lecture 07, let's look at slide 7: Nested Grouping SymbolsâExamples 3E and 3F: Part E: 3 + 2[ 5x - 4(x - 9) ] + 7. Sora, when grouping symbols are nested inside brackets, how do we proceed? Work strictly from the INSIDE OUT!\n\n[TA Sora] Always locate the innermost parentheses first. Here, the innermost part is -4(x - 9). Distribute -4: (-4)(x) = -4x, and (-4)(-9) = +36. Now look inside the brackets: [ 5x - 4x + 36 ]. Combine like terms inside the brackets: 5x - 4x = x.\n\n[Prof. Park] So the brackets collapse to [ x + 36 ]! Look how clean that is! Now our expression is: 3 + 2[ x + 36 ] + 7. Next, distribute the outside 2 into the brackets: 2(x) = 2x, and 2(36) = 72.\n\n[TA Sora] Now our full line is: 3 + 2x + 72 + 7. Now simply combine all the constants: 3 + 72 = 75, plus 7 = 82! So our final simplified answer is: 2x + 82.\n\n[Prof. Park] Look at how an intimidating problem with brackets, parentheses, and four operations collapsed into a simple two-term binomial: 2x + 82! When students try to rush and distribute from the outside in, they create enormous fractions and sign errors. Working from the inside out guarantees clarity at every step.\n\n[TA Sora] In Lecture 08, we advance to Section 1.4: Multiplying Polynomials and the FOIL Method on workbook page 14. Practice combining like terms tonight, and we will see you in Lecture 08! Let us emphasize one final time the difference between adding terms and multiplying terms:\n\n[Prof. Park] When adding like terms: 3x^2 + 5x^2 = 8x^2. The coefficients add, but the exponent NEVER changes! When multiplying: (3x^2)(5x^2) = 15x^4. The coefficients multiply and the exponents ADD! Confusing these two rules is the number one cause of lost points on Exam 1!\n\n[TA Sora] Take your time with every step here. Writing out intermediate lines is the secret to 100% accuracy!\n\n[TA Sora] Exactly! Protect your signs, trust the algebra rules, and you will get the correct answer every single time!"
