    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Example: } & (x + 3)(x + 5) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot x + x \\cdot 5 + 3 \\cdot x + 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= x^2 + 5x + 3x + 15 = \\mathbf{x^2 + 8x + 15}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Sora's Warning:** FOIL is just a memory aid for the distributive property. When multiplying a binomial by a trinomial, FOIL expands to 6 multiplications!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Welcome everyone to Lecture 08 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me is TA Sora. Today, we explore Section 1.4 of our course workbook, spanning pages 14 and 15: Multiplying Polynomials and the FOIL Strategy. Hello everyone!\n\n[TA Sora] Today we learn how to multiply binomials and polynomials together. This is one of the most famous and heavily used procedures in all of secondary and college mathematics.\n\n[Prof. Park] Whether you are finding the area of a newly planned building footprint in Bozeman, calculating economic revenue functions (price times quantity) for a business in Belgrade, or factoring quadratic equations in Unit 2, polynomial multiplication is the universal engine behind it all. That is right, Sora.\n\n[TA Sora] When we multiply two binomialsâlike (a + b)(c + d)âevery term in the first binomial must multiply every term in the second binomial. To help students remember all four multiplications without missing any, educators developed the famous acronym: FOIL! F stands for First terms. O stands for Outer terms.\n\n[Prof. Park] I stands for Inner terms. L stands for Last terms. You can also think of FOIL geometrically as an area model or a 2-by-2 Punnett square: draw a rectangle partitioned into 4 smaller cells. The top sides are a and b, and the left sides are c and d.\n\n[TA Sora] The areas of the four cells are ac, ad, bc, and bd! When you sum the cells together, you get the exact same result. Visual learners in architectural drafting and carpentry often love the box model, while algebraic thinkers love the rhythmic flow of FOIL.\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's examine how the FOIL strategy works on slide 2 with Example 1! Imagine you are designing a high-performance energy-efficient modular home for a client building in the Bridger Foothills.\n\n[TA Sora] If one room module has length (2x + 5) feet and width (3x - 4) feet, calculating the total square footage requires finding the product of those two binomials. Every square foot of flooring, insulation, and solar radiant heating depends on correctly multiplying these algebraic dimensions!\n\n[Prof. Park] That is why polynomial multiplication is not an abstract puzzleâit is physical reality in construction and architecture."
