    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Step 1: } & \\text{Find LCD of all denominators.} \\\\\n\\text{Step 2: } & \\text{Multiply numerator and denominator of each fraction by missing factor.} \\\\\n\\text{Step 3: } & \\text{Combine numerators over the single common denominator.} \\\\\n\\text{Step 4: } & \\text{Simplify if possible.}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Sora's Warning:** NEVER add denominators! $\\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{2}{y}$, NOT $\\frac{2}{2y}$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Welcome everyone to Lecture 09 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora. Today, we launch into Section 1.5 of our course workbook, found on page 16: Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions. Hello everyone!\n\n[TA Sora] Today represents a major milestone in your algebra journey. We are taking the fraction arithmetic skills we mastered back in Lectures 01 and 02 and merging them with the variable polynomials we have studied all week.\n\n[Prof. Park] A rational expression is simply a fraction where the numerator and denominator are polynomialsâlike 5 over w, or 3 over x squared.\n\n[TA Sora] Students are often intimidated when variables appear downstairs in the denominator, but the fundamental law of fractions NEVER changes: to add or subtract pieces, they must share a common denominator! That is the universal principle: A/C + B/C = (A + B)/C, and A/C - B/C = (A - B)/C.\n\n[Prof. Park] If the denominators already match, you simply add or subtract the numerators and keep the common denominator. Think of physical units in everyday Montana life: imagine agricultural irrigation ditches in the Gallatin Valley.\n\n[TA Sora] If you have one canal pumping 3 cubic feet of water per second through a diversion gate of capacity C, and a second canal feeding 2 cubic feet per second through that same gate capacity C, the combined flow rate is (3 + 2) = 5 cubic feet per second over that identical gate capacity C!\n\n[Prof. Park] You never add the gate capacities together to get 2C! You only add the amounts of water flowing through them. Exactly! But if the denominators are differentâlike one pump measured in cubic feet per second and another in gallons per minuteâyou cannot combine them directly.\n\n[TA Sora] You must find the Least Common Denominator, or LCD. Finding the LCD for algebraic expressions follows a simple, foolproof two-step strategy that works every single time: Step 1: Find the numerical LCD for the integer coefficients using standard times tables.\n\n[Prof. Park] For example, for 6 and 4, the numerical LCD is 12. Step 2: For each variable that appears in any denominator, take that variable raised to its HIGHEST exponent present in any single denominator!\n\n[TA Sora] For instance, if one denominator has x and another has x cubed, the LCD must contain x cubed. Once you have the LCD, you scale each fraction by multiplying top and bottom by whatever factors are missing, combine the numerators over the common denominator, and reduce to lowest terms.\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's see this in action on slide 2! In Gallatin Valley irrigation management, ditch riders control the flow of water to alfalfa fields and cattle pastures.\n\n[TA Sora] If headgate A delivers flow at rate 5/(6x^2) and headgate B delivers flow at rate 3/(8x), you cannot simply add the 5 and 3 to get 8, and add the denominators to get 14x^3! That would be like adding two fractions with different units without converting them first!\n\n[Prof. Park] In physics and engineering, you must find a common standard unit before combining rates. In algebra, that common unit is the Least Common Denominator."
