    "script": "[Prof. Park] To conclude Lecture 09, let's examine slide 5 with Examples 2E and 2F: Mixed Constant and Variable Denominators: Part E: 2/(7r) + 3/(14r). Part F: 5/(6k^2) - 1/(4k). In Part E, look at the denominators: 7r and 14r.\n\n[TA Sora] Notice that 14 is a direct multiple of 7 (7 Â· 2 = 14)! And both have r to the first power. Therefore, our LCD is simply 14r! The second fraction 3/(14r) stays unchanged.\n\n[Prof. Park] We only need to scale the first fraction: multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (2 Â· 2) / (7r Â· 2) = 4 / (14r). Now add: 4/(14r) + 3/(14r) = (4 + 3) / (14r) = 7 / (14r). But wait! Look at our result: 7 / (14r).\n\n[TA Sora] Are we finished?\n\n[TA Sora] Absolutely not! Always inspect your final fraction for reduction opportunities! 7 on top and 14 on the bottom both divide by 7! 7 divided by 7 is 1. 14 divided by 7 is 2. So 7/(14r) reduces cleanly to 1 / (2r)!\n\n[Prof. Park] Remember: the 1 in the numerator is mandatory. You cannot write 2râthe 2r is in the denominator, so it must be 1/(2r). Now let's conquer Part F: 5/(6k^2) - 1/(4k). This is our grand finale for Lecture 09! Coefficients: 6 and 4.\n\n[TA Sora] Multiples of 6 are 6, 12... multiples of 4 are 4, 8, 12... The numerical LCD is 12! Variables: k^2 and k^1. The highest power is k^2. So our total LCD is 12k^2!\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's scale both fractions to 12k^2: For 5/(6k^2), multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (5 Â· 2) / (6k^2 Â· 2) = 10 / (12k^2). For 1/(4k), to turn 4k into 12k^2, we must multiply by 3k!\n\n[TA Sora] Multiply top and bottom by 3k / 3k: (1 Â· 3k) / (4k Â· 3k) = 3k / (12k^2). Now subtract the numerators: (10 - 3k) / (12k^2). Can we reduce 10 - 3k over 12k^2? No, 10 and 3k share no common factor, so nothing can be factored out.\n\n[Prof. Park] Our final answer is: (10 - 3k) / (12k^2)! In Lecture 10, we continue Section 1.5 with advanced rational expressions: higher powers, three-term sums, and binomial numerators. See you all in Lecture 10! Notice the beauty of our final fraction in Example 3: (21b - 4a^2) / (30 a^3 b^2).\n\n[TA Sora] Could we cancel the b in 21b with b^2 in the denominator? No! Because the term -4a^2 does not have a b! If a factor is not shared by EVERY term in the numerator, it cannot be cancelled!\n\n[Prof. Park] That is the ironclad rule of rational expressions: all terms in the numerator must share the factor before anything can cancel across the fraction bar!"
