    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 2 presents Section 1.5 Example 3A on workbook page 17: Add and simplify: 3 / (7x^2) + 5 / (14x). Let's find our Least Common Denominator systematically: Look at the numerical coefficients: 7 and 14. 14 is a multiple of 7, so our numerical LCD is 14.\n\n[TA Sora] Look at the variable powers: x^2 and x^1. The highest power is x^2. Combine them: our LCD is 14x^2! Now let's check what each fraction needs: The first fraction is 3 / (7x^2). Its denominator already has x^2, but needs its coefficient scaled from 7 to 14.\n\n[Prof. Park] So we multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (3 Â· 2) / (7x^2 Â· 2) = 6 / (14x^2). The second fraction is 5 / (14x). Its coefficient is already 14, but it only has x to the first power. It needs one more x to reach x^2!\n\n[TA Sora] So we multiply top and bottom by x/x: (5 Â· x) / (14x Â· x) = 5x / (14x^2). Now both fractions share the identical denominator 14x^2.\n\n[Prof. Park] Combine the numerators: 6/(14x^2) + 5x/(14x^2) = (6 + 5x) / (14x^2), which we write in standard order as (5x + 6) / (14x^2). Can we cancel x in 5x with x^2 on the bottom? Never! 5x is part of the sum (5x + 6).\n\n[TA Sora] Unless the entire binomial has a common factor with the denominator, no canceling is allowed. (5x + 6) / (14x^2) is our final answer!\n\n[Prof. Park] Here is my personal study tip for workbook page 17: whenever you see a problem like (A + B)/C - (D + E)/C, draw bright red parentheses around (D + E) before you write another stroke of your pencil!\n\n[TA Sora] That single second of drawing protective parentheses eliminates 90% of all student errors on rational expression exams. Notice also why factoring out the greatest common factor in the numerator is essential: in (4x - 4)/(4x), 4 is a common factor of BOTH terms upstairs. Factoring gives 4(x - 1).\n\n[Prof. Park] That allows the 4 to cancel with the 4 downstairs. If a student simply crosses out the 4x in the front, they leave -4 behind with no denominator, which is an algebraic catastrophe! Always factor before you cancel! No factoring, no cancelling! That is our golden motto!"
