    "script": "[Prof. Park] Let's elevate the challenge with Example 3B on slide 3: a three-term rational expression with a 4th power: Simplify: 2/x - 9 / (10x^4) + 3 / (5x^2). Three fractions at once! Don't let the length intimidate youâour LCD strategy works identically regardless of how many fractions there are.\n\n[TA Sora] Look at the denominators: x, 10x^4, and 5x^2. Step 1: Numerical coefficients are 1, 10, and 5. Multiples of 10 and 5 give a numerical LCD of 10! Step 2: Variable powers are x^1, x^4, and x^2. The highest exponent present is 4! So our variable LCD is x^4.\n\n[Prof. Park] Combining them gives our overall Least Common Denominator: 10x^4! Now let's scale each of the three fractions into equivalent fractions with denominator 10x^4: Fraction 1: 2/x. To turn x into 10x^4, multiply by 10x^3: (2 Â· 10x^3) / (x Â· 10x^3) = 20x^3 / (10x^4). Fraction 2: 9 / (10x^4).\n\n[TA Sora] Its denominator is already 10x^4! It needs no scaling at all. Fraction 3: 3 / (5x^2). To turn 5x^2 into 10x^4, multiply by 2x^2: (3 Â· 2x^2) / (5x^2 Â· 2x^2) = 6x^2 / (10x^4).\n\n[Prof. Park] Now combine all three numerators over the common denominator 10x^4, paying close attention to the operations: The first operation is subtraction, and the second is addition: (20x^3 - 9 + 6x^2) / (10x^4). Rearrange the numerator terms in descending degree order: (20x^3 + 6x^2 - 9) / (10x^4).\n\n[TA Sora] None of the numerator terms can be combined because their degrees are 3, 2, and 0. And 20, 6, and -9 share no common factor with 10. Our three-term rational expression is fully simplified! Look closely at how we simplified (3x + 6) / (3x^2) on slide 3:\n\n[Prof. Park] Notice that we factored out 3 from BOTH terms in the numerator: 3(x + 2). Because 3 is multiplied by (x + 2), 3 is a FACTOR! And 3 in the denominator is also a FACTOR!\n\n[TA Sora] Nowâand ONLY nowâcan the 3 cancel top and bottom, leaving (x + 2) / (x^2)! Factoring first is the only legal ticket to cancelling in rational expressions!"
