    "script": "[Prof. Park] Moving to slide 4, let's examine Examples 3C and 3D: Multi-Term Common Denominators: Part C: 2 / (3b) + 1/4 + 1 / (6b). Sora, walk us through finding the LCD when one term is a pure constant fraction without a variable! That is a wonderful detail to notice!\n\n[TA Sora] The denominators are 3b, 4, and 6b. First, look at the numbers: 3, 4, and 6. Multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18... 12 is divisible by 3, 4, and 6! So our numerical LCD is 12.\n\n[Prof. Park] Second, look at the variable: b appears to the first power in two denominators. Even though the middle fraction 1/4 has no b, any fraction in the family that needs b must receive b! So our variable LCD is b. Combining them gives an LCD of 12b!\n\n[TA Sora] Let's scale all three fractions to 12b: Fraction 1: 2 / (3b). Multiply top and bottom by 4/4: (2 Â· 4) / (3b Â· 4) = 8 / (12b). Fraction 2: 1/4. To turn 4 into 12b, we must multiply by 3b!\n\n[Prof. Park] Multiply top and bottom by 3b / 3b: (1 Â· 3b) / (4 Â· 3b) = 3b / (12b). Fraction 3: 1 / (6b). Multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (1 Â· 2) / (6b Â· 2) = 2 / (12b).\n\n[TA Sora] Now combine the numerators over 12b: 8 + 3b + 2 = (3b + 10) / (12b). Can we reduce 3b + 10 over 12b? Look at 3, 10, and 12: 3 and 12 share 3, but 10 is not divisible by 3.\n\n[Prof. Park] 10 and 12 share 2, but 3 is not divisible by 2. There is NO common factor shared by all terms. Therefore, (3b + 10) / (12b) is in lowest terms!\n\n[TA Sora] On slide 4, look at the distribution of scaling factors: when multiplying (x + 3) by 3, you must distribute 3 to BOTH x and 3: 3x + 9! And when multiplying (2x - 1) by 2, you must distribute 2 to BOTH 2x and -1: 4x - 2!\n\n[Prof. Park] Never leave a term behind when building equivalent fractions upstairs! In Example 3C, notice the factored form of the final answer: 7(x + 1) / (12x). On exams, both 7(x + 1)/(12x) and (7x + 7)/(12x) are 100% acceptable.\n\n[TA Sora] Factoring out the 7 lets you quickly check whether anything can cancel with the denominator 12x. Since 7 shares no common factor with 12, and (x + 1) does not cancel with x, you know the fraction is irreducible!\n\n[Prof. Park] That is why factoring your final answer gives you an instant built-in correctness check!"
