    "script": "[Prof. Park] Welcome everyone to Lecture 11 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora.\n\n[TA Sora] Today, we cross a major milestone in our course: we begin Section 1.6 on workbook pages 18 and 19: Solving Linear Equations and the Properties of Equality. Hello everyone! Up until today, we have been working with algebraic expressionsâsimplifying them, adding them, and multiplying them.\n\n[Prof. Park] Notice that an expression has NO equal sign! You cannot solve an expression; you can only simplify it. Today, for the first time, we encounter an EQUATION: two algebraic expressions joined by an equal sign!\n\n[TA Sora] An equation is a complete mathematical sentence that states: 'The left side has the EXACT same numerical value as the right side!'\nThat distinction is foundational.\n\n[Prof. Park] Think of an equation as a traditional mechanical balance scale, like the antique gold and grain scales used during the Montana gold rush in Virginia City and Alder Gulch. If you have equal weights on both pans, the scale balances perfectly horizontally.\n\n[TA Sora] If you add 5 pounds to the left pan, the scale tipsâunless you immediately add the exact same 5 pounds to the right pan!\n\n[Prof. Park] That gives us our Golden Rule of Equation Solving: Whatever you do to one side of the equation, you MUST do to the other side! That is the universal law of algebra! Let's state our two core properties: 1.\n\n[TA Sora] Addition and Subtraction Property of Equality: If a = b, then a + c = b + c, and a - c = b - c. Adding or subtracting the exact same number from both sides maintains complete equality. 2.\n\n[Prof. Park] Multiplication and Division Property of Equality: If a = b and c is not zero, then a Â· c = b Â· c, and a / c = b / c. Multiplying or dividing both sides by the exact same non-zero number maintains complete equality.\n\n[TA Sora] And what is our ultimate goal when solving an equation? Our goal is to isolate the variable completely by itself on one side: x = [a number].\n\n[Prof. Park] To isolate the variable, we undo the operations that are attached to it using INVERSE operations in reverse order: Undo addition with subtraction; undo subtraction with addition; undo multiplication with division; and undo division with multiplication.\n\n[TA Sora] Let's put this into practice on slide 2 with our first two-step equation! In the history of mathematics, the word 'algebra' comes from the 9th-century Arabic treatise 'Al-Kitab al-mukhtasar fi hisab al-jabr wal-muqabala' by the Persian mathematician Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi.\n\n[Prof. Park] The word 'al-jabr' literally meant 'the restoration of broken parts' or 'setting a broken bone'âre-balancing two sides of a scale! When an equation is out of balance, algebra provides the precise surgical operations to restore absolute equilibrium. That historical root is so inspiring!\n\n[TA Sora] Whenever students ask me why algebra is so strict about doing the exact same thing to both sides, I tell them: think of flying a backcountry bush plane across the Absaroka Range.\n\n[Prof. Park] If you load 50 pounds of survival gear onto the left wing, you MUST balance the aircraft by loading 50 pounds onto the right wing, or your plane will bank into the trees! Equations obey the exact same law of physical balance."
