    "script": "[Prof. Park] On slide 2, let's examine Section 1.6 Example 1A on workbook page 18: Solve the linear equation for the given variable: 3x - 8 = -12. Look at the variable x on the left side. What is happening to x?\n\n[TA Sora] Two operations are attached to x: First, x is multiplied by 3. Second, 8 is subtracted from that result. When solving equations, we undo operations in REVERSE order of operationsâreverse PEMDAS! Think of getting dressed in the morning: you put your socks on first, then your boots.\n\n[Prof. Park] When taking them off at night, what comes off first? The boots! So we undo the addition or subtraction first, and save the multiplication or division for the very last step! What a brilliant analogy, Sora!\n\n[TA Sora] Let's take off the boots first: To undo the subtraction of 8, we add 8 to BOTH sides of the equation: 3x - 8 + 8 = -12 + 8. On the left side: -8 + 8 = 0, leaving just 3x.\n\n[Prof. Park] On the right side: -12 + 8 = -4! So our equation simplifies to: 3x = -4. Now take off the socks: x is multiplied by 3. The inverse of multiplying by 3 is dividing by 3!\n\n[TA Sora] Divide BOTH sides by 3: (3x) / 3 = (-4) / 3. 3 / 3 is 1, leaving x isolated: x = -4/3! Notice that our answer is an improper fraction in simplest form.\n\n[Prof. Park] Do NOT convert it to a decimal like -1.333 unless the problem explicitly asks for a decimal. In algebra, -4/3 is exact, elegant, and standard.\n\n[TA Sora] Now let's perform our mandatory check: plug x = -4/3 back into the original equation: 3(-4/3) - 8 = -4 - 8 = -12. -12 = -12 is a true statement! That proves with 100% mathematical certainty that x = -4/3 is the correct unique solution!\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's discuss why we reverse the order of operations when solving equations. In Section 1.0, we learned PEMDAS: Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division, Addition and Subtraction. PEMDAS is for EVALUATING when you already know all the numbers and want to find the final value.\n\n[TA Sora] Solving an equation is the exact REVERSE process: you already know the final value (-12), and you are working backward through time to discover what mystery number x created it! Exactly!\n\n[Prof. Park] It's like wrapping a birthday gift: you put the toy in a box (multiplication), wrap it in paper (subtraction), and tie a bow. When unwrapping the gift, you don't break the toy firstâyou untie the bow, unwrap the paper (add 8), and open the box (divide by 3)!\n\n[TA Sora] Reverse order of operations is the natural, logical unwrapping of an algebraic gift!"
