    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 3 introduces the notorious 'Negative Coefficient Trap': Section 1.6 Example 1B on workbook page 18: Solve: 6 - 4w = 15. Danger alert! Look at the left side: 6 - 4w. Students see the minus sign after 6 and mistakenly think the 6 is negative!\n\n[TA Sora] They say: 'I should add 6 to both sides.' That is completely wrong! The number 6 has an invisible positive sign in front of it: +6! The minus sign belongs to the term 4w! So the coefficient of w is NEGATIVE 4!\n\n[Prof. Park] That is one of the most common errors in all of introductory algebra. To eliminate the constant +6, we must SUBTRACT 6 from both sides: 6 - 4w - 6 = 15 - 6. On the left: 6 - 6 = 0, leaving -4w!\n\n[TA Sora] Notice the minus sign did NOT disappearâit stays attached to 4w! On the right: 15 - 6 = 9. So our equation is: -4w = 9. Now to isolate w, we must divide both sides by the ENTIRE coefficient of w, which is -4!\n\n[Prof. Park] Divide both sides by -4: (-4w) / (-4) = 9 / (-4). Negative divided by negative is positive: w = -9/4! If a student had forgotten that the 4 was negative, they would have divided by +4 and gotten +9/4, losing points.\n\n[TA Sora] Always circle the sign in front of the coefficient to remind yourself that the sign travels with the number! Let's check w = -9/4: 6 - 4(-9/4) = 6 - (-9) = 6 + 9 = 15. 15 = 15! It checks out perfectly. Always guard your signs!\n\n[Prof. Park] Here is an exam horror story from my first year as a TA: On a midterm question almost identical to 6 - 4w = 15, over 50% of the class wrote 4w = 9 instead of -4w = 9!\n\n[TA Sora] They saw 6 - 4w and treated the minus sign like a decorative hyphen! A minus sign in algebra is never decorative! It is a negative charge that binds directly to the term immediately following it.\n\n[Prof. Park] If you ever feel tempted to ignore that sign, rewrite the equation in standard form using the Commutative Property of Addition: -4w + 6 = 15! When you see -4w + 6 = 15, no one ever forgets that the 4 is negative!\n\n[TA Sora] Rearranging terms to put the variable first is a bulletproof test-taking strategy."
