    "script": "[Prof. Park] On slide 4, we examine Section 1.6 Examples 1C and 1D on workbook page 18: Equations where you must combine like terms on the same side BEFORE moving across the equal sign!\n\n[TA Sora] Here is another vital distinction: If two like terms are on the SAME side of the equal sign, you do NOT use inverse operations! You simply combine them using regular addition and subtraction from Section 1.3!\n\n[Prof. Park] You only use inverse operations when moving a term ACROSS the equal sign to the other side! Let's solve Example 1C: y + 4 + 5y - 9 = 87. Look at the left side: we have y and +5y.\n\n[TA Sora] They are like terms! y + 5y = 6y. We also have constants +4 and -9: 4 - 9 = -5. So the left side simplifies to: 6y - 5 = 87. Look how simple that is now!\n\n[Prof. Park] It's just a standard two-step equation: Add 5 to both sides: 6y = 87 + 5 = 92. Divide both sides by 6: y = 92/6. Both 92 and 6 are even numbers, so divide top and bottom by 2: y = 46/3! That is our simplified solution.\n\n[TA Sora] Now let's solve Example 1D: 6r - 3r + 94 + 2r = 18. Combine all r terms on the left side: 6r - 3r = 3r, and 3r + 2r = 5r! So the left side becomes: 5r + 94 = 18.\n\n[Prof. Park] Subtract 94 from both sides: 5r = 18 - 94 = -76. Divide both sides by 5: r = -76/5! 76 is not divisible by 5, so -76/5 is our final irreducible answer.\n\n[TA Sora] Combining like terms first cuts the complexity of an equation in half before you ever touch an inverse operation!\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's emphasize Sora's 'Border Crossing Rule' for slide 4: Look at Example 1C: y + 4 + 5y - 9 = 87. y and 5y are both on the left side of the border (the equal sign). Because they are on the same side, they don't need passports!\n\n[TA Sora] You simply combine them: 1y + 5y = 6y! Students who try to subtract 5y from both sides here end up with a double-subtraction catastrophe! That's right! You only use inverse operations when crossing the international border of the equal sign!\n\n[Prof. Park] If terms are inside the same country, just group them together normally like old friends. Organize your own house first before knocking on your neighbor's door!"
