    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 5 concludes Lecture 11 with Section 1.6 Examples 2A and 2B on workbook page 18: Solving Equations involving the Distributive Property. When an equation contains parentheses, our first priority is to eliminate parentheses using the Distributive Property.\n\n[TA Sora] Let's look at Example 2A: 10(a - 5) + 2 = -3. Step 1: Distribute 10 across (a - 5): 10 Â· a - 10 Â· 5 = 10a - 50. So our equation is: 10a - 50 + 2 = -3.\n\n[Prof. Park] Step 2: Combine like terms on the left side: -50 + 2 = -48. So we have: 10a - 48 = -3. Step 3: Add 48 to both sides: 10a = -3 + 48 = 45. Step 4: Divide both sides by 10: a = 45/10.\n\n[TA Sora] Reduce by dividing top and bottom by 5: a = 9/2! Outstanding! Now let's tackle Example 2B, which has variables on BOTH sides of the equation: 6(x - 2) - 8 = 3x - 20.\n\n[Prof. Park] Step 1: Distribute the 6 on the left: 6x - 12 - 8 = 3x - 20. Step 2: Combine like terms on the left: -12 - 8 = -20. So we get: 6x - 20 = 3x - 20. Step 3: Move variable terms to one side.\n\n[TA Sora] Subtract 3x from both sides: 6x - 3x - 20 = -20, which gives: 3x - 20 = -20. Step 4: Add 20 to both sides: 3x = -20 + 20 = 0! Step 5: Divide both sides by 3: x = 0 / 3 = 0!\n\n[Prof. Park] Students often freeze when they see zero and think something went wrong. Zero is a perfectly valid, real number! If x = 0, let's check: Left: 6(0 - 2) - 8 = 6(-2) - 8 = -12 - 8 = -20.\n\n[TA Sora] Right: 3(0) - 20 = 0 - 20 = -20. -20 = -20! It checks completely. The solution is x = 0! In Lecture 12, we will look at equations where variables cancel out completelyâidentities and contradictions.\n\n[Prof. Park] Great job today, and we look forward to seeing you in Lecture 12!\n\n[TA Sora] In Example 2B, notice what happened when we reached 3x = 0: Many students panic and write: 'No solution' or 'Undefined.' Let's be 100% clear: 0 divided by 3 is ZERO: 0 / 3 = 0! Dividing zero by a number is completely legal and equals zero!\n\n[Prof. Park] Dividing by zeroâlike 3 / 0âis what is undefined! Zero is a perfectly respectable integer! If the temperature outside in Bozeman during January drops to exactly 0Â° Fahrenheit, that does NOT mean the thermometer broke or that temperature has no solution! It means the temperature is zero!\n\n[TA Sora] Always embrace x = 0 as a proud, valid solution!"
