    "problem": "### Solve each formula for r (Workbook p. 24)\n$$\\text{C. } m = \\frac{r - 7n}{5}; \\quad \\text{solve for } r$$\n$$\\text{D. } t = \\frac{d}{r}; \\quad \\text{solve for } r$$",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{Part\\ C:}\\quad & \\text{Step 1: Multiply both sides by } 5: \\\\\n& 5m = r - 7n \\\\\n& \\text{Step 2: Add } 7n \\text{ to both sides:} \\\\\n& \\mathbf{r = 5m + 7n} \\\\[1em]\n\\mathbf{Part\\ D:}\\quad & \\text{Target } r \\text{ is in the denominator! Multiply by } r \\text{ first:} \\\\\n& r \\cdot t = d \\\\\n& \\text{Now divide by } t: \\\\\n& \\mathbf{r = \\frac{d}{t}}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Variable in Denominator Trap:** In 2D, you CANNOT solve for $r$ while it is trapped in the bottom! Multiply by $r$ first to bring it upstairs: $rt = d \\implies r = \\frac{d}{t}$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 5 presents Section 1.8 Examples 2C and 2D on workbook page 24: Solving fractional formulas for r: Part C: m = (r - 7n) / 5; solve for r. Part D: t = d / r; solve for r.\n\n[TA Sora] Look at Part C: m = (r - 7n) / 5. Our target is r, but r is trapped inside a fraction! How do we free r from the fraction? Clear the fraction!\n\n[Prof. Park] Multiply both sides by the denominator 5: 5 Â· m = 5 Â· [ (r - 7n) / 5 ]. On the right, the 5 cancels, leaving: 5m = r - 7n. Now add 7n to both sides to isolate r: r = 5m + 7n!\n\n[TA Sora] Multiplying by the denominator first immediately freed our variable! Now look at Part D: t = d / r. Solve for r. This is the famous distance, rate, and time formula from physics: time = distance / rate!\n\n[Prof. Park] For example, driving 90 miles on Interstate 90 from Bozeman to Butte at speed r takes time t. Notice where r is: r is trapped DOWNSTAIRS in the denominator! You can NEVER solve for a variable while it is in the denominator!\n\n[TA Sora] Step 1: Get r out of the basement! Multiply both sides by r: r Â· t = r Â· (d / r) = d. So we get: rt = d (distance = rate times time).\n\n[Prof. Park] Step 2: Now that r is upstairs, isolate r by dividing both sides by t: r = d / t! Speed equals distance divided by time. Clean, intuitive, and physically meaningful! Let's look at the physics formula in Part D: t = d / r.\n\n[TA Sora] If you drive from Bozeman to Billings on Interstate 90, the distance d is about 142 miles. If you want to make the trip in t = 2 hours, what average speed r must you maintain?\n\n[Prof. Park] Using our solved formula r = d / t: r = 142 / 2 = 71 miles per hour! Formulas give you direct control over real physical decisions. And notice the common pitfall: students see t = d/r and write r = d Â· t!\n\n[TA Sora] Remember: since r was in the denominator, multiplying by r brings it to the numerator on the other side: rt = d. Then dividing by t gives r = d/t!"
