    "problem": "### Solve each geometry formula for W (Workbook p. 24)\n$$\\text{E. } P = 2L + 2W; \\quad \\text{solve for } W$$\n$$\\text{F. } V = LWH; \\quad \\text{solve for } W$$",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{Part\\ E\\ (Perimeter):}\\quad & \\text{Step 1: Subtract } 2L \\text{ from both sides:} \\\\\n& P - 2L = 2W \\\\\n& \\text{Step 2: Divide by } 2: \\\\\n& \\mathbf{W = \\frac{P - 2L}{2}} \\quad \\left(\\text{or } \\mathbf{W = \\frac{P}{2} - L}\\right) \\\\[1em]\n\\mathbf{Part\\ F\\ (Volume):}\\quad & \\text{Divide both sides by } LH: \\\\\n& \\mathbf{W = \\frac{V}{LH}}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Don't Cancel the 2 Illegally!** In Part E, $\\frac{P - 2L}{2} \\neq P - L$! The 2 divides the ENTIRE numerator, so it becomes $\\frac{P}{2} - L$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] On slide 6, we explore geometric formulas from workbook page 24: Part E: P = 2L + 2W; solve for W. Part F: V = LWH; solve for W. Part E is the perimeter formula for a rectangle: Perimeter = 2 times Length + 2 times Width.\n\n[TA Sora] Imagine fencing a horse pasture in the Gallatin Valley: you know the total perimeter fencing P you bought, and you know the pasture length L. How wide can the pasture be? We must solve for W!\n\n[Prof. Park] Step 1: Isolate the 2W term by subtracting 2L from both sides: P - 2L = 2W. Step 2: Divide both sides by 2: W = (P - 2L) / 2 (or W = P/2 - L).\n\n[TA Sora] Can the 2 in 2L cancel with the 2 in the denominator? NO! P is not multiplied by 2! You cannot cancel unless BOTH terms share the factor 2! Now look at Part F: V = LWH. Solve for W.\n\n[Prof. Park] This is the volume formula for a rectangular prism (like a timber beam or concrete foundation footer): Volume = Length times Width times Height. W is multiplied by both L and H: (LH) Â· W = V.\n\n[TA Sora] To isolate W, divide both sides by LH: W = V / (LH)! Width equals volume divided by the product of length and height. In Part E: P = 2L + 2W; solve for W.\n\n[Prof. Park] Let's see the pasture fencing example with real numbers: Suppose a Gallatin rancher has P = 1,000 feet of wooden post fencing and wants the pasture length to be L = 300 feet.\n\n[TA Sora] Using our formula W = (P - 2L) / 2: W = (1000 - 2(300)) / 2 = (1000 - 600) / 2 = 400 / 2 = 200 feet! The pasture width must be 200 feet. Formula rearranged once, solved in ten seconds!\n\n[Prof. Park] That is the beauty of applied mathematics."
