    "num": 7,
    "type": "math_problem",
    "slideTypeLabel": "Official Workbook Problem",
    "title": "Section 1.8 Review: Solving Equations by Clearing Fractions",
    "subtitle": "Unit 1 • Lecture 14 • Section 1.8 Review A & B (Workbook p. 24)",
    "detail": "Lecture 14: Solving Formulas for a Specified Variable (Literal Equations)",
    "instructor": "Prof. Eunju Park • TA Sora (Gallatin College MSU)",
    "problem": "### REVIEW: Solve the following equations by clearing fractions (Workbook p. 24)\n$$\\text{A. } \\frac{y - 2}{5} = \\frac{3}{2}y + \\frac{4}{5}$$\n$$\\text{B. } \\frac{2}{7} = \\frac{3}{7}(x - 4) - \\frac{5}{7}$$",
    "solution": "$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{Review\\ A:}\\quad & \\text{Denominators: } 5, 2 \\implies \\mathbf{\\text{LCD} = 10} \\\\\n& 10 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{y - 2}{5}\\right) = 10 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}y\\right) + 10 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\\\\n& 2(y - 2) = 15y + 8 \\\\\n& 2y - 4 = 15y + 8 \\implies -12 = 13y \\implies \\mathbf{y = -\\frac{12}{13}} \\\\[1em]\n\\mathbf{Review\\ B:}\\quad & \\text{Denominators are all } 7 \\implies \\mathbf{\\text{LCD} = 7} \\\\\n& 7 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right) = 7 \\cdot \\left[\\frac{3}{7}(x - 4)\\right] - 7 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right) \\\\\n& 2 = 3(x - 4) - 5 \\\\\n& 2 = 3x - 12 - 5 \\implies 2 = 3x - 17 \\implies 19 = 3x \\implies \\mathbf{x = \\frac{19}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}$$",
    "pitfall": "**Multiplying Binomial Numerator:** In Review A, $10 \\div 5 = 2$, which must distribute to $(y - 2)$ to make $2y - 4$!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 7 concludes Lecture 14 with our Section 1.8 Review Problems from workbook page 24: Solving equations by clearing fractions: Review A: (y - 2) / 5 = (3/2)y + 4/5. Review B: 2/7 = (3/7)(x - 4) - 5/7.\n\n[TA Sora] Let's solve Review A: (y - 2) / 5 = (3/2)y + 4/5. Look at the denominators: 5, 2, and 5. The LCD is 10!\n\n[Prof. Park] Multiply every term by 10: 10 Â· [ (y - 2) / 5 ] = 10 Â· [ (3/2)y ] + 10 Â· [ 4/5 ]. First term: 10 / 5 = 2, so 2(y - 2) = 2y - 4. Second term: 10 Â· (3/2) = 15y.\n\n[TA Sora] Third term: 10 Â· (4/5) = 8. So we get: 2y - 4 = 15y + 8. Subtract 2y from both sides: -4 = 13y + 8. Subtract 8 from both sides: -12 = 13y. Divide by 13: y = -12/13!\n\n[Prof. Park] Now let's solve Review B: 2/7 = (3/7)(x - 4) - 5/7. All denominators are 7, so the LCD is 7! Multiply every term by 7: 2 = 3(x - 4) - 5. Distribute: 2 = 3x - 12 - 5.\n\n[TA Sora] Combine like terms: 2 = 3x - 17. Add 17 to both sides: 19 = 3x. Divide by 3: x = 19/3! In Lecture 15, we conclude Unit 1 with Section 1.9: Solving Linear Inequalities and Interval Notation! Fantastic work today, and see you in Lecture 15!\n\n[Prof. Park] In Review Problem A: (y - 2)/5 = (3/2)y + 4/5, we multiplied by LCD = 10 to get 2(y - 2) = 15y + 8, leading to y = -12/13.\n\n[TA Sora] Notice how literal equations and clearing fractions reinforce each other: Both skills require identifying the structural anatomy of an equation and systematically peeling away outer operations. In Lecture 15, we will conclude Unit 1 with inequalities!"
