    "script": "[Prof. Park] Welcome everyone to Lecture 15 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora.\n\n[TA Sora] Today is a celebration: we complete Section 1.9 on workbook pages 25 through 27: Solving Linear Inequalities, Graphing on Number Lines, and Interval Notationâand we conduct our Grand Unit 1 Review! Hello everyone!\n\n[Prof. Park] Today we transition from equations (where two sides are equal) to INEQUALITIES (where one side is strictly greater than, less than, or equal to the other side). In real life, conditions are rarely exact equalities.\n\n[TA Sora] When civil engineers design the bridge over the Gallatin River on Interstate 90, the load limit is an inequality: Weight â¤ 80,000 pounds. When backcountry skiers check avalanche danger at Bridger Bowl, snowpack temperature must stay below freezing: T < 32Â° Fahrenheit.\n\n[Prof. Park] Today, we learn how to solve inequalities, express their infinite solution sets on number lines, and write them in standard INTERVAL NOTATION!\n\n[TA Sora] And we must introduce the single most famous rule in all of inequality algebra: The Golden Rule of Inequalities: When you MULTIPLY or DIVIDE both sides of an inequality by a NEGATIVE number, you MUST REVERSE (FLIP) the inequality symbol!\n\n[Prof. Park] If it was less than, it becomes greater than! If it was greater than or equal to, it becomes less than or equal to! Sora, explain why this flip happens with simple numbers. Look at 2 and 5. We all agree that 2 < 5.\n\n[TA Sora] Now multiply both numbers by -1: 2 becomes -2, and 5 becomes -5. Which is larger: owing 2 dollars or owing 5 dollars? -2 is larger than -5! So -2 > -5! The symbol FLIPPED from less-than to greater-than!\n\n[Prof. Park] Multiplying by a negative reflects the numbers across zero to the opposite side of the number line, reversing their relative order! Remember: only flip the symbol when you multiply or divide by a NEGATIVE number. Let's begin on slide 2!\n\n[TA Sora] Let's reflect on the number line to understand why the inequality symbol flips: Imagine a giant mirror sitting right at the origin, zero: x = 0.\n\n[Prof. Park] If you are standing at +3 and your friend is standing at +7, your friend is farther to the right: 3 < 7. Now reflect both of your positions across the mirror by multiplying by -1: You are now at -3, and your friend is at -7!\n\n[TA Sora] On the negative side of the mirror, who is farther to the right? YOU ARE! -3 is closer to zero and farther right than -7! So -3 > -7!\n\n[Prof. Park] Multiplying by a negative literally flips the entire universe of numbers across the mirror, turning right into left and left into right! That mirror analogy makes it impossible to forget! Whenever a negative number multiplies or divides across an inequality, the world flips!"
