    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 2 covers Section 1.9 Examples 1A and 1B from workbook page 25: Basic Interval Notation and Number Line Graphing: Part A: x > 5. Part B: x < -6.\n\n[TA Sora] Let's master our notation conventions: Strict inequalities (< and >) use an OPEN circle on the graph and PARENTHESES ( ) in interval notation because the boundary endpoint is NOT included!\n\n[Prof. Park] Inclusive inequalities (â¤ and â¥) use a CLOSED (solid) circle or square bracket [ ] because the boundary endpoint IS included! And infinity (â) and negative infinity (-â) ALWAYS take parentheses ( ) because infinity is a direction, not a reachable number!\n\n[TA Sora] Now let's graph Part A: x > 5. On the number line, place an open parenthesis at 5 and shade everything to the RIGHT toward positive infinity. In interval notation, read from left to right: (5, â). Now look at Part B: x < -6.\n\n[Prof. Park] Place an open parenthesis at -6 and shade everything to the LEFT toward negative infinity. Reading from left to right: (-â, -6). Always write the smaller (leftmost) number first in interval notation!\n\n[TA Sora] Let's review interval notation formatting: Interval notation ALWAYS reads from left to rightâfrom smaller numbers to larger numbers! Never write (â, 5) or (-6, -â)! Negative infinity is the furthest possible destination to the left, so it must ALWAYS be on the left: (-â, -6).\n\n[Prof. Park] Positive infinity is the furthest destination to the right, so it must ALWAYS be on the right: (5, â)!\n\n[TA Sora] Remember our classroom motto: discipline with steps yields effortless accuracy on exams!\n\n[TA Sora] Exactly! Take it line by line, check each arithmetic move, and celebrate every victory!"
