    "problem": "**Essential Unit 3 Formulas:**\n\n$$\\textbf{Standard Form: } f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c$$\n\n$$\\textbf{Vertex Form: } f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k$$\n\n$$\\textbf{Axis of Symmetry: } x = \\dfrac{-b}{2a}$$\n\n$$\\textbf{Quadratic Formula: } x = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\n$$\\textbf{Discriminant: } \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$$",
    "solution": "**Quick Reference:**\n\n| Feature | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Vertex x | $\\dfrac{-b}{2a}$ |\n| Vertex y | $f\\left(\\dfrac{-b}{2a}\\right)$ |\n| y-intercept | $(0, c)$ |\n| x-intercept | Solve $f(x)=0$ |\n| Discriminant | $b^2-4ac$ |\n| Domain | $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ always |\n| Range (up) | $[k,\\infty)$ |\n| Range (down) | $(-\\infty,k]$ |",
    "pitfall": "**Sora’s Study Tip:** Write these formulas on an index card and put it on your desk. By the end of this semester, you’ll have them memorized without even trying!",
    "script": "[Prof. Park] Slide 6 is your M090 Formula CardÃÂ¢ÃÂÃÂkeep this forever in your academic career! \n\n[TA Sora] It contains: The General Form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, Vertex Form $a(x - h)^2 + k$, Vertex Formula $x_v = -b / (2a)$, Quadratic Formula $x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, Discriminant $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$, and the factoring special forms!\n\n[Prof. Park] These formulas are the building blocks for College Algebra, Precalculus, Business Calculus, and Engineering Calculus.\n\n[TA Sora] Keep this Formula Card on slide 6 forever! 1. Standard Form: f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c. 2. Vertex: x = -b / (2a), y = f(-b / (2a)). 3. Vertex Form: f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k with vertex (h, k). 4. Quadratic Formula: x = [ -b plus or minus sqrt(b^2 - 4ac) ] / (2a). 5. Discriminant: Delta = b^2 - 4ac (positive: 2 roots, zero: 1 root, negative: 0 real roots). These five formulas are the permanent foundation for all college algebra, precalculus, and calculus!\n\n[Prof. Park] When you walk into your next mathematics courseâwhether Math 121 College Algebra or a statistics courseâthis Formula Card will be your trusted companion. Every professor will expect you to recognize standard form, calculate the vertex with x = -b/(2a), and deploy the quadratic formula with confidence. You have mastered these tools completely.\n\n[TA Sora] You have built a powerhouse foundation that will carry you through college algebra, precalculus, and beyond! Be proud of your hard work and keep that momentum going strong!\n\n[Prof. Park] As we reach the conclusion of Unit 3, think about the immense mathematical journey you have completed: from parabolas and factoring to quadratic formula derivations!",
